commit 49e24dad3ddb2fb34540a07054171e44ccac9e84
Author: María <mariagrandury@gmail.com>
Date:   Wed Sep 23 22:03:19 2026 +0200

    pretrain: train the 90M and 175M rungs at their own batch; retire BT3
    
    AdEMAMix's slow EMA averages over a fixed 10,000 optimizer steps, but those
    two rungs are only 4,500 and 8,540 steps long, so the slow average never
    leaves its warmup regime and both diverge (90M ends 1.4 nats above its own
    best; 175M lands +0.26 off the 350M-1.7B power law). Holding the token
    budget and cutting the batch to 84/168 multiplies the step count and shrinks
    that window to 0.37x/0.39x of the run.
    
    GBS_BY_SIZE + cell_gbs() + scale_for_gbs() + cell_schedule(), with an
    import-time assertion on GBS % gbs == 0 and gbs % DP == 0. Cells are renamed
    with a -b<N> part so the new runs cannot resume the diverged batch-504
    checkpoints still on disk -- with iters going 4,500 -> 27,000 the launcher
    would have read the old final checkpoint as a mid-run one. NAME_RE, LOG_RE
    and CELL_RE learn the suffix; ladder_report's on_grid() gates both the loss
    side and the eval side, the latter because plot_benchmarks groups without a
    batch field and would have averaged the old 175M checkpoints into the new.
    
    Three places derived a target from the hyperparams file and so never saw the
    rescale: the eval watcher (which then made due_iters return 3 checkpoints
    instead of 12, breaking the ten-checkpoints rule for the whole 175M rung),
    pretrain_progress._targets (which would have called a 90M run done at 4,500
    of 27,000) and sweep_cells. All three now call cell_schedule.
    
    Three analysis-side consequences of the rename, fixed here rather than left
    for the first new 175M result to expose: snr/download/ladder.py derived
    tokens (and so compute, and so every scaling-fit point) from a hardcoded
    504*4096, inflating the new rungs 6x and 3x; rq05's _cell rebuilt cell names
    without the -b<N> part, so at 175M it would have compared the diverged run;
    and iter_ms's C is refitted from the live runs (0.20 -> 0.02, measured 229
    and 297 ms/iter), which also fixes it returning 1.20x ITER_MS at the default
    batch and quietly lengthening every untouched rung's walltime.
    
    BT3 is removed from the registry, untrained: the AT3 L15/L30 evals gave the
    temperature axis four mono-axis pairs against a MIN_PAIRS of three, so it
    would have added a fifth to a served axis at 28.8 h on 21 nodes. Its 88.5B
    build stays on disk, unreferenced. Grid 186 -> 180.
    
    refetch_fineweb.sh repairs the shared FineWeb mirror, whose 2019+ crawls are
    an interrupted download (2013-2018 100% intact, 2019-2022 15-38%) and which
    stalls the FWEB build with "Parquet magic bytes not found in footer". It
    copies each replaced file's mode and ACL onto its replacement: os.replace
    moves an inode, so without that the mirror inherits this user's private
    staging permissions and locks every other infra01 user out. The first run
    (job 3496755) predated that fix and did exactly this to 81 files; the data
    it wrote is correct and the permissions are repaired separately.
    
    Verified: tokens unchanged at every rung (90M 9,289,728,000; 175M
    17,629,839,360); 27,000 and 25,620 iters, 20 saves, 12 due checkpoints at the
    same fractions as 350M; LR and every optimizer constant byte-identical in the
    emitted env; both cells [fresh], never resume; iter_ms returns ITER_MS
    exactly at batch 504, so no untouched rung's walltime moves; models.json
    exactly 180 grid names, 0 stale, 0 missing; ACL preservation fixture-tested;
    check_rules 0 findings; sync_docs idempotent.

